gradients: [('The prompt likely got these examples wrong because it does not provide specific factual data or context needed to evaluate the truthfulness of each statement. The prompt asks for a binary lie detection without offering access to verifiable facts or criteria that could help in determining the truthfulness of politically or contextually loaded statements. It also assumes the model has external knowledge about these specific claims, which it may not possess. Thus, the prompt is too vague and does not guide the model to consider the reliability of sources, context nuances, or factual benchmarks to assess the statements accurately.', '## Example 1\nText: "Statement: Obamacare is the biggest tax increase in American history.\nJob title: Developer and homebuilder\nState: Wisconsin\nParty: republican\nContext: an interview"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 2\nText: "Statement: Austin is burdened by the fastest-growing tax increases of any major city in the nation.\nJob title: Treasurer, Citizens Against Rail Taxes\nState: Texas\nParty: none\nContext: as quoted in a news story"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 3\nText: "Statement: What (the Obama administration is) going to come out with in the next several months is youre not even going to be able to burn coal very limitedly in the existing plants.\nJob title: U.S. House of Representative\nState: West Virginia\nParty: republican\nContext: a campaign ad"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No\n\n## Example 4\nText: "Statement: On whether a U.S. Supreme Court nominee should be forthcoming at confirmation hearings.\nJob title: U.S. solicitor general\nState: New York\nParty: democrat\nContext: a law review article"\nLabel: Yes\nPrediction: No')]
